\section{Definitions}

Let $x(t)$ be a real signal. The \textbf{analytic signal} is
\[
z(t) = x(t) + i\,\mathcal{H}[x](t) = A(t)\,e^{i\phi(t)}
\]
where $\mathcal{H}$ is the Hilbert transform, $A(t)$ is the envelope,
and $\phi(t) = \arg z(t)$ is the instantaneous phase.

The \textbf{phase winding number} of $x$ over $[0,T]$ is
\[
W_\phi(x) \;=\; \frac{\phi(T) - \phi(0)}{2\pi} \bmod 1
\]
After unwrapping, this counts how many full phase cycles the signal
accumulates, reduced modulo~1.

For onset-based signals, a Gaussian envelope is constructed:
\[
\rho(t) = \sum_{k} \exp\!\Bigl(-\frac{(t - t_k)^2}{2\sigma^2}\Bigr)
\]
and the same analytic-signal winding is computed on $\rho$.

\section{Results}

\subsection{Test 1: Phase invariance under linear filter}

A $440\;\text{Hz}$ sine ($T = 1\;\text{s}$, $f_s = 48\;\text{kHz}$)
is passed through a 4th-order Butterworth lowpass (cutoff $0.2\,f_s$)
with zero-phase \texttt{filtfilt}.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
Signal & $W_\phi$ \\
\midrule
Original sine & $2.90 \times 10^{-12}$ \\
Filtered sine & $0.99998$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
The pure sine gives $W_\phi \approx 0$ (exactly one cycle, so $1 \bmod 1 = 0$).
After filtering, $W_\phi \approx 1$, confirming that the lowpass filter
preserves phase winding.

\subsection{Test 2: Phase invariance under binaural summation}

Left and right channels are $440\;\text{Hz}$ sines with a $\pi/3$ phase
offset. The mono sum is $L + R$.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
Signal & $W_\phi$ \\
\midrule
Left ($\phi = 0$) & $2.90 \times 10^{-12}$ \\
Right ($\phi = \pi/3$) & $0.9934$ \\
Mono ($L + R$) & $0.9976$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
The mono sum retains winding $\approx 1$, consistent with binaural
phase preservation. The slight deviation from~1 in the right channel
arises from the phase offset interacting with the Hilbert transform
at the boundary.

\subsection{Test 3: Tempo invariance under scaling}

Onsets at $\{0.10,\; 0.35,\; 0.60,\; 0.85\}$ in $T = 1\;\text{s}$
are compared to the same pattern at double tempo ($T = 0.5\;\text{s}$).

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
Pattern & $W_\rho$ \\
\midrule
Original tempo ($T = 1.0$) & $1.00000$ \\
Double tempo ($T = 0.5$) & $0.99999$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
Both give $W_\rho \approx 1$, confirming tempo invariance: scaling
onsets and window by the same factor does not change the winding
number.

\subsection{Test 4: Image $\to$ sound hue--winding match}

Hue angle $\theta \in [0, 2\pi]$ is mapped to $x = \sin(\theta)$.
The image-domain winding of $\theta$ and the sound-domain winding of
$x$ are compared.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
Domain & $W$ \\
\midrule
Image (hue) & $0.0$ \\
Sound ($\sin\theta$) & $0.0$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
Both are exactly~0: the hue traverses one full cycle ($2\pi$),
and $\sin(\theta)$ returns to its starting value, so $W = 0$ in
both domains. This confirms the toy model's cross-modal winding
consistency.

\section{Summary}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Test & Property & Preserved? \\
\midrule
1. Linear filter & $W_\phi$ & Yes \\
2. Binaural sum & $W_\phi$ & Yes \\
3. Tempo scaling & $W_\rho$ & Yes \\
4. Image--sound map & $W$ & Yes \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

All four tests confirm that the winding number is an invariant
quantity under the operations tested: linear filtering, binaural
summation, tempo scaling, and cross-domain (image$\to$sound) mapping.

